Confirmed. The bug is on line 142: `if (!job.workCountries.length) return "yes";`. When the candidate has no work countries on record (or when no job is passed at all -> workCountries is `[]`), this returns "yes" -> "Yes - I am authorized to work where this role is based." This is a positive claim without evidence, exactly the fabrication AGENTS.md forbids, and exactly what the bug report describes.
`reachAllows` on the core side does the opposite: line 372 `if (!workCountries.length) return "unknown";` (but only after the open check). The comment in conservative-default.ts even says the named country decides, and the fallback to "hires anywhere" means the candidate works from home and is authorized there - but that premise requires knowing where home is. With zero work countries, we don't know anywhere.
Fix: return "unknown" when workCountries is empty.
And in the negative branch, the `why` text for `unknown` currently says "Where the posting is based cannot be read from it, and authorization is a document." - this is inaccurate when the profile has no work countries (the posting may well be readable). Add a third case to the why chain: if there are no work countries on record, say so.
Also update the doc comments for whereTheWorkIs (lines 136-138) and the doc comment for conservativeDefaultAnswer (lines 152-159). The latter says "Absent it, that branch still answers - with the negative, because a positive with nothing behind it is the fabrication this module exists to prevent." - actually this doc is already correct. It's the code that contradicts it.
Now, the implementation:
```ts
function whereTheWorkIs(job: JobReach): "yes" | "no" | "unknown" {
const places = namedPlaces(job.location, job.remoteScope);
if (!job.workCountries.length) return "unknown";
if (places.countries.length) {
return places.countries.some((c) => job.workCountries.includes(c))
? "yes"
: "no";
}
if (places.open) return "yes";
return reachAllows(places, job.workCountries) === "yes" ? "yes" : "unknown";
}
```
Wait, let me double-check the "open -> yes" branch when workCountries is non-empty. Doc: "only a posting that names none falls back to 'hires anywhere', which means the candidate works from home and is authorized there." This is based on the premise that the candidate works legally where they live (consistent with the OWN_COUNTRY and RESIDENCE branches answering yes). When workCountries is non-empty we preserve the existing behavior. When empty -> unknown (no evidence about home). Good.
Also, since we early-return when workCountries is empty, `reachAllows(places, job.workCountries)` on line 149 now never sees an empty array - no problem.
And the negative branch:
```ts
const work = job ?? { location: null, workCountries: [] };
const reach = whereTheWorkIs(work);
if (reach === "yes") { ... }
return {
answer: "No - I am not authorized to work in the country where this role is based.",
kind: "boolean",
why:
reach === "no"
? "The posting is based where the candidate has no right to work."
: !work.workCountries.length
? "The profile holds no work countries, and authorization is a " +
"document. The negative claims nothing; the positive would be a " +
"fabrication."
: "Where the posting is based cannot be read from it, and " +
"authorization is a document. The negative claims nothing; the " +
