};
```
Hmm, wait: `job ?? { location: null, workCountries: [] }` - the JobReach interface requires `location` and `workCountries`, with remoteScope optional. The existing code on line 206 already does exactly this, so keep the same pattern. Just rename it to a variable so we can check the length.
Doc comment on whereTheWorkIs - the final paragraph says "So: a named country decides; only a posting that names none falls back to 'hires anywhere', which means the candidate works from home and is authorized there." Add: "and a profile that holds no work countries at all decides nothing, because then even 'home' is unknown."
Next, the tests to add (they must fail without the fix and pass with it):
1. Greenhouse question + a candidate with no work countries + US job location -> expect No (fails without the fix: currently returns Yes).
2. Greenhouse question + no work countries, job location where the candidate can work... actually, the bug: no work countries + "Anywhere in the World" worldwide -> currently yes, after the fix -> No.
3. No job passed at all + no work countries -> currently: job is undefined -> workCountries is [] -> "yes". After fix -> No.
4. Also, no job passed at all with a non-empty workCountries... currently: job ?? { location: null, workCountries: [] } -> empty -> yes. Hmm interesting: if the caller passes no job at all, even a Brazilian candidate gets "yes". That's also wrong - no job data -> we can't know where the job is located -> unknown -> negative. With my fix, the empty workCountries from the fallback -> unknown -> negative. Covered by the same fix. Good - the bug report said "also when the caller passes no job at all."
